['Here is a simple implementation of atoi() function in C, along with a main function to demonstrate its usage with command line arguments:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int my_atoi(char* str) {
    int sign = 1, number = 0;
    if (*str == '-') {
        sign = -1;
        str++;
    }
    for (; *str >= '0' && *str <= '9'; str++) {
        number = number * 10 + (*str - '0');
    }
    return sign * number;
}

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    if (argc < 2) {
        printf("Usage: %s argument\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    int num = my_atoi(argv[1]);
    printf("%s converted to integer is: %d\n", argv[1], num);
    return 0;
}
```

With this code, if you run `./a.out foo bar baz`, argc would be 4, and argv[0] would be "./a.out". If you run it with an integer as the argument, like `./a.out 123`, then argc would be 2, argv[0] would be "./a.out", and argv[1] would be "123". The my_atoi() function will convert the string "123" to the integer 123, and the main function demonstrates how to use this function with command line arguments.']

