[' Here's a C function that implements the requirements you've specified. The function `scan` reads a string from the standard input and stores it in an array of size 1023, leaving space for the null terminator.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int scan(char str[1024]) {
    int i = 0;
    char c;

    // Read characters until a newline or the end of the buffer is reached
    while ((i < 1023) && (scanf("%c", &c) == 1)) {
        if (c != '\n') {
            str[i++] = c;
        }
    }

    // Ensure the string is null-terminated
    str[i] = '\0';

    return i;
}
```

The function `scan()` uses a while loop to read characters from standard input one at a time. If a character is not a newline, it is stored in the array `str`. The loop continues until either the buffer is full or a newline character is encountered. Finally, the string is null-terminated and the number of characters read (excluding the newline character) is returned.']

